    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Cataclastic pseudotachylyte-bearing faults of the Gole Larghe Fault Zone provide an excellent opportunity to characterize the mechanical balance of frictional heat production, fluid diffusion, and pore dilation during seismic events in the middle crust (~9- 11km). Fault thickness and relative displacement can be measured along a number of exhumed faults, and some examples provide evidence of single-slip-events of 1-1.5 m. Wall rock along cataclastic faults ranges from completely intact to severely damaged. We use this information to simulate coupled temperature, pressure, and pore volume evolution during seismic slip on a representative, idealized fault. Measurements of fault thickness, total slip, permeability, and pore space isothermal and isobaric expansivities in the field and laboratory allow for characterization of dynamic variables such as temperature rise, thermal pressurization, pore dilation, and shear stress drop during slip along ancient faults.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
